Subject: Re: Integration kickoff: SDK + endpoint migration plan

On Mon, Nov 1, 2026 at 11:45 AM Alex Rivera <alex@pioneeranalytics.com> wrote:
> Marissa — thanks for the clear summary. Quick answers from my side:
> - Environments: dev (dev.pioneeranalytics.internal), staging (staging.pioneeranalytics.com), prod (api.pioneeranalytics.com)
> - Expected peak: ~120 concurrent generation requests, typical batch sizes are single-shot for chat and up to 16 for embeddings
> - Network: we have an outbound proxy; we can add IP ranges but prefer to authenticate via mTLS or key rotation if supported
>
> Two questions before we schedule the dry-run:
> 1) For retries/backoff, should we implement request-idempotency markers or rely on the SDK to de-dupe?
> 2) Token rotation — can the SDK hot-swap keys without restarting client processes?

Hi Alex — great questions. Adding detail below and pasting a minimal sample from the quickstart for easy copy/paste.

1) Idempotency and retries- We recommend implementing idempotency keys for any non-idempotent operations (most generation calls are non-idempotent if they mutate state). For plain text generation and embeddings, you can safely retry a failed network call if you attach an idempotency-key header. The Redwood SDK exposes a convenience field 'idempotencyKey' in the request object. Sample pattern: generate -> if 429 or transient 5xx then retry with exponential backoff up to 4 attempts; bubble 4xx except 429.

2) Token rotation and auth- The SDK supports hot key rotation via the token provider interface. If you wire a local secret manager that implements the provider interface, you can rotate keys without restarting — the SDK will refresh on the next request. If you use mTLS, you'll need to rotate certs at the process level but can automate via your orchestration system.

Minimal sample (Node.js) — paste from redwood_sdk_quickstart.md:

const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = new Redwood.Client({ tokenProvider: myTokenProvider, timeout: 60000 });

async function safeGenerate(prompt, idKey) {
  const maxAttempts = 4;
  let attempt = 0;
  while (attempt < maxAttempts) {
    try {
      attempt++;
      return await client.generate({ prompt, idempotencyKey: idKey, stream: false });
    } catch (err) {
      if (err.status === 429 || (err.status >= 500 && err.status < 600)) {
        const backoff = Math.min(30000, 200 * Math.pow(2, attempt)) + Math.random() * 2000;
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, backoff));
        continue;
      }
      throw err;
    }
  }
  throw new Error('Max retries reached');
}

3) Rate limiting- On the client side we recommend a token-bucket limiter tuned to your average request rate with a burst capacity of 2x expected peak. On the server side you should handle 429s as an instructive signal to back off; we also expose a 'retry-after' header where applicable.

I attached the quickstart and the migration checklist to this thread again. Happy to hop on Tuesday — propose 11:00 AM PT.

